  [rainy-footsteps-wet-pavement] grader rejected: At defaults the waveform after the heel strike is a regular, evenly spaced oscillation lasting about 0.3 s. It reads as a struck resonant body, like a low tom or a plucked box, not a shoe meeting wet concrete. A wet step should be a broadband slap followed by irregular, noisy splash and squelch detail. | The spectrum is too dark for rain-slick pavement. The centroid is about 470-600 Hz for every shoe except heel, and most of the energy sits on a low band at the bottom of the spectrogram. Little bright splash or spray noise appears in the 2-8 kHz region, where 'wet' would be heard. | The maximum-range render makes the ringing worse. It shows about 15 clean decaying cycles, close to a drum hit, so the weight and squelch extremes push the sound further toward the wrong kind of sound instead of a heavier, wetter step. | Seed 3 is strongly periodic and ringing while the default seed is noisier. The two takes differ in character, not just in detail, so the ringing appears to be an unstable artifact rather than a designed component. | With the tail on, a single step lasts about 0.73 s with roughly 40% silence. The decay drags on longer than a dry step needs, and the extra length is resonant ringing, not wet ambience or drip. | With tail = false the waveform shows several lumpy secondary bursts. These read more like a wobble than a clean scuff or squelch. | The 'step rate' knob does little for a one-shot step render. A designer would rather have a wetness or splash-amount control, or a puddle-depth control, that adds bright spray noise.
  [rainy-footsteps-puddle] grader rejected: Sneaker and heel are effectively the same sound. Centroid is 2708 vs 2711 Hz, with near-identical envelopes and spectrograms. A heel on wet ground should have a hard, narrow, bright click at the attack, and a sneaker a soft rubbery slap. Here both are the same broadband splash, so the headline shoe choice does not deliver. | The impact and splash are one undifferentiated ~0.3 s broadband noise wash. There is no distinct sole contact followed by the water displacement, so the step reads more as 'something splashed' than a foot landing in a puddle. | Roughly 40-50% of every render is trailing near-silence after the last droplet. Editors will have to trim the dead tail on every placement. | The all-minimum and all-maximum renders differ only modestly in brightness and level. The splash depth and weight extremes do not produce the shallow-tap versus heavy-stomp contrast the knob names promise. | The droplet tail is sparse and a little random-blippy, with isolated ticks scattered out to ~0.45 s. It lacks a convincing short spatter cluster right after the impact.
